# TODO: Empire 6

Plans for Empire 6 (`modules/i.empire.lbl`, `modules/i_emp.*.lbl`; the game's sources and notes are in `games/empire6/`). The rest of Image 128's list is `modules/TODO.md`.

- [ ] Empire 6 on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal (user, 11/22/2026; `empire` branch): when stations share the drive and more than one caller is playing, offer things that happen between them as they play. (0) First, and needed even without these: `e.empire6` is one REL file every station reads and writes (a player's save, combat rewriting the enemy's record, maintenance), so take an `&,13` lock around each read-modify-write (PROGRAMMING.md, "`&,13`"); likewise `e.empnews6`. (1) Who's playing: each station writes its player number (`g3`) and handle into a small shared file, record = station (port 0-15), cleared on leaving or at logoff, or ask `++ chatmode`'s planned quiet query (`&,16,5`, the entry above) who's online and match handles. (2) Realtime battles: attacking a player who is on now sends them a challenge, shown at their next prompt; the defender can answer within a time limit (send reserves, pay tribute, sue for peace, or ride out to joust, as the tourney does), each side's move through a shared exchange record under a lock; no answer in time, or the defender not on, and the battle is fought as now. (3) Chat: an Empire channel, `++ chatmode`'s room with only the players in it ("the war council"), from the game's menu; and one-line messages to one ruler (a taunt, a treaty offer) shown at their next prompt. (4) A herald: news as it happens; when a player on another station wins a quest, enrages Zircon or takes someone's land, a one-line notice reaches the other players at their next prompt, not only in the news file next call. (5) Live trading: offer another ruler who is on now grain, timber, spices or horses (the new stats) for gold or land; both have to accept before anything moves, under the lock, so neither can spend it twice. (6) The sysop joins in (user, 11/22/2026), at any station's console, as `++ chatmode` lets them into its room by turning `Cht` on: in the chat channel (3); in battles (2), taking a side live as one of the game's own figures: playing Lord Randall (the computer's ruler, `en$="RANDALL"`) when a player attacks him, or King George stepping in on a battle (reinforcements, a truce, or a ruling); and as the herald (4), announcing events of their own. (7) Watching a battle: other players on now follow a live battle as spectators, and can bet on it in Lucifer's Den (the Town). (8) Joint quests: two rulers on now go on a quest together (Empire II's quests, `i/emp.quests`), with better odds than alone and the reward shared. Testing needs two stations: see the Lt. Kernal in x128 entry above ("two x128s on one image file aren't a multiplexer")
- [ ] Empire 6: alliances and treaties (user, 11/22/2026; `empire` branch; for any BBS, players on different calls): two rulers sign a pact, no attacks on each other for N days, or a shared defence (an ally's soldiers come to your aid when you're attacked). Signing one takes a diplomat (`f%(6)`, which nothing uses yet). Breaking it costs nobles' loyalty and goes in the news ("<name> breaks the treaty!"). Combat checks the pact before an attack. Needs a pacts file (two players, the kind, until when)
- [ ] Empire 6: mail between rulers (user, 11/22/2026): offers, threats and treaty proposals left for another player, shown at the start of their next turn, with Accept/Refuse for an offer or a treaty (the alliances entry above)
- [ ] Empire 6: tribute and vassals (user, 11/22/2026): a ruler who loses badly can choose to become the winner's vassal rather than lose land, paying a share of their taxes to the winner each turn until they buy their freedom; shown on both rulers' status screens and in the news
- [ ] Empire 6: a market between players (user, 11/22/2026): sell orders that wait ("500 horses at $40") for another ruler to buy on a later call; the seller is paid at their next turn. Gives timber, spices, horses and cannons (fields 17-21, added for the quests) a use beyond the quests. Writes to the market file take an `&,13` lock on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal
- [ ] Empire 6: use the new stats (user, 11/22/2026): forts and cannons add to defence in combat, horses to attack, timber goes into building ships and palaces, spices are traded or keep serfs content (fewer leave or riot). Raids can aim at a store ("steal their spices"). Today `hs tb sp cn fo` are only won in quests and shown on the status screen
- [ ] Empire 6: spies and sabotage (user, 11/22/2026): pay to see another ruler's stats (today Y shows only your own), or to sabotage them (burn a mill, poison grain). Each attempt can fail, and a caught spy makes the news. Diplomats or a new "spies" count could carry it out. Scrying (user, 11/22/2026): a seer in the Town (a new item on its menu, beside Lucifer's Den and Utopia's Auction) gazes into a crystal ball, for a fee, at another ruler's realm: their stats as on Your Status, or a part of them for less (army, stores, buildings). No spy to lose, but the vision can be cloudy (a figure off by some percent, more for a cheap gazing or a ruler with many forts), and the seer might tell the other ruler who asked (in the news: "<name> was seen in the crystal")
- [ ] Empire 6: sieges (user, 11/22/2026): an attack on a ruler with forts takes several of the attacker's turns; the defender sees "Under siege by <name>" at their next login and can send relief (soldiers, grain) or surrender. Needs a sieges file (attacker, defender, turns left). Planning them on a map: the siege planning entry below
- [ ] Empire 6: houses (user, 11/22/2026): players join one of two or three houses (teams), each with a treasury its members pay into and draw from, and a house rank in the news and on the wall
- [ ] Empire 6: barbarian invasions (user, 11/22/2026): now and then a horde (a strength figure) threatens every ruler; each player who sends soldiers wears it down, and the contributors share the reward when it's beaten. If it isn't beaten in time, every ruler loses land. Its progress shows at each login and in the news
- [ ] Empire 6: seasons and a winner (user, 11/22/2026): a season ends after N weeks or when a ruler reaches Emperor; the Hall of Fame records the winner (Empire II's `emp.fame`) and the game resets. This puts the config file's auto-reset settings to use (`ln`: on the land requirement, `mp`: on Emperor status; `e.emp 6 config`, games/empire6/variables.txt)
- [ ] Empire 6: a tournament ladder (user, 11/22/2026): the joust (`i/emp.tourney`) between real players, with challenges, standings posted on the Empire wall, and the champion in the news
- [ ] Empire 6: bounties (user, 11/22/2026): a player posts BBS credits (`cr`) on a ruler's head, paid to whoever defeats them (in combat, or by taking their palace). Builds on the credit/money exchange in Empire 6's config (`en`, `nn`)
- [ ] Empire 6: crops and farmers (user, 11/22/2026): grow more than "grain". Now the harvest is one figure (i/emp.trading 111: `x=int((la*wc+rnd(1)*sf*wc+gr*wc)/3)`, capped at `(la+sf)*4`; `wc` the weather) and the people eat 5 bushels each (`sf*5+1`). Instead: crops the medieval world had (not potatoes, which reached Europe from the Americas only around the 1570s): wheat, rye, barley and oats (bread, ale, horse feed: horses, `hs`, eat oats), peas and beans, turnips and cabbages, onions, and cash crops, flax (linen) and hops (ale) and grapes (wine) to sell. Each takes acres you choose to plant, and does better or worse in different weather (rye and oats stand cold and wet, wheat wants a good summer; spreading the planting is the hedge against a bad year), with a rotation bonus for leaving land fallow or in beans. A new stat, farmers (taken from the serfs, as soldiers are): the harvest per acre rises with the farmers working it, so more farmers feed the people better, up to what the land needs; too few and crops rot in the fields. The record gets the new fields (as horses-forts did: i.empire's save/load, startup, combat's enemy copy, maintenance's editor; 200 bytes holds a few more numbers, then the length goes up again, the file remade by Reset Empire)
- [ ] Empire 6: markets with goods (user, 11/22/2026): a market sells things, not only a sum (now each earns money from the serf count: i/emp.endturn 31, `p2=int(((rnd(1)*75)+sf/100)*f%(2))`). Kinds of goods: the crops above (flax, hops, wine), cloth and linen, salt, iron and tools (from the foundries), timber and spices (`tb`, `sp`), horses (`hs`), wool and leather; each with a price that moves with supply (what all the rulers hold) and the season. Buying goods you lack and selling your surplus, between turns; with the market between players (the entry above), other rulers' goods too
- [ ] Empire 6: Utopia's Auction between players (user, 11/22/2026): the Town's auction (i/emp.town 287-298) now sells you Utopia's own serfs at the price the game sets (`wb`). Expand it to auction serfs (and slaves, the Slave Market's 2,500) between rulers: a player puts some up with a reserve price, others bid on later calls (or live, on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal), and the highest bid when the auction closes takes them; Utopia keeps a cut, and still "hates greed" (her guards for a bid that's too low)
- [ ] Empire 6: the ruler's gender, courtship and marriage (user, 11/22/2026): ask a new ruler's gender, and use it for titles (the ranks, `d2$`: Lord or Lady, Duke or Duchess, King or Queen, Emperor or Empress) and in the texts (he/she; they for a ruler who'd rather). Courtship between rulers (players, or the game's own nobility for a ruler alone): gifts, letters (the mail between rulers entry), attending each other's festivals and banquets earn courtship points; enough of them and a proposal can be made and accepted. A marriage joins two realms in an alliance (the treaties entry) and the dowry passes at the wedding: gold, land, grain or goods, set by the bride's or groom's family as agreed. Weddings in the news and on the wall; an heir later, perhaps. Needs fields for gender, spouse (player #) and courtship points (record length: see the crops entry)
- [ ] Empire 6: a court wizard (user, 11/22/2026): hire one (a yearly wage, like the soldiers' pay) who casts spells, each with a cost (gold, spices, a rare reagent from a quest) and a chance to fail or backfire: bless the harvest (better weather for a year), ward off plague or rats, a fog over the army (an attack's odds), scry (the Town seer's spell, cheaper), curse a rival's grain store or granary, summon a storm on an enemy fleet. A rival's wizard can counter. Zircon (quest 9) could be the wizard's master: a wizard who's served long enough asks to study with him
- [ ] Empire 6: festivals and banquets (user, 11/22/2026): hold a festival (a harvest feast, a saint's day, a tourney fair, the ruler's wedding) or a banquet, paid for in gold, grain, wine and spices: it raises the people's mood (fewer flee or riot, more immigrants: the census), the nobles' loyalty, and courtship points. Invite other rulers: on any BBS the invitation waits for them, and they attend on their next call (gifts exchanged, news); on a multiplexed Lt. Kernal, rulers on now come in person, with a live hall (the Empire chat channel), toasts, a joust for the guests, and the sysop as a guest of honor or as King George. Plenty of flavor text: menus (roast swan, venison pasties, eel pie, subtleties of sugar), minstrels and jesters, a guest who drinks too much or insults the host, a poisoned cup now and then (with a food taster to hire)
- [ ] Empire 6: siege planning (user, 11/22/2026; goes with the sieges entry): before attacking a ruler's palace, plan the siege on a map of their castle, in Commodore color graphics (C/G callers; a text map for ASCII): several castle maps (a motte-and-bailey, a concentric castle, a hilltop keep, a castle on a lake or by the sea), each with walls, towers, gates, a moat, a keep. Choose where to place the camp and the engines: trebuchets and mangonels (stones, flaming pitch, or diseased carcasses over the wall), battering rams under a shelter at the gate, siege towers rolled to a wall (the moat filled first, with timber and earth), scaling ladders, and sappers mining under a tower to bring it down (the defender can countermine). Also: cutting off the water or food and starving them out (days, against their grain store), a night attack, bribing a guard to open a postern gate, Greek fire or boiling oil on the defender's side, and the weather (rain stops fires and bogs down towers). The engines cost timber (`tb`), the attack horses and cannons (fields 17-21), and the defender's forts (`fo`) and wall repairs count against them. The siege plays out over the attacker's turns, shown on the map as it goes (breaches, fires, towers taken)
